const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Patient journey &amp; insights","serviceType":"Data service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/patient-journey-insights-machine-learning/","description":"Real-world care pathways reconstructed from EHR, claims, wearables and patient-reported data.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How does machine learning change patient journey analysis?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It makes it possible at all on the volumes involved. Linking millions of records across sources, reconstructing sequences, and reading free text are not tasks that scale by hand.\n\nWhat it does not change is inference. These are observational data, so what comes out is association and sequence, not causation — and any analysis that blurs that is telling you something it cannot know."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What data goes into journey mapping?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Electronic health records, clinical trial data, wearables and mobile apps, patient surveys and feedback, claims and billing data, and digital health platforms.\n\nThe linkage is usually the hard part. Match rates are never 100%, and the patients who fail to link are rarely a random sample — so the report states the rate and who was lost."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who uses this?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pharmaceutical and biotech companies, hospitals and providers, medical device manufacturers, public health organisations, payers and insurers, CROs and HTA agencies.\n\nThe output differs a good deal between them. A hospital wants the intervals in its own pathway; a market access team wants model inputs with their uncertainty attached."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the main benefits?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Understanding disease progression as it actually occurs, locating where adherence fails, informing R&D and trial design with real-world behaviour, and optimising treatment pathways against evidence rather than assumption.\n\nThe single most common useful finding is an interval nobody had measured — typically time from first symptom to diagnosis, and where it is being spent."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How is patient data protected?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The lawful basis, the consent position and where processing may occur are settled before any data moves. De-identification to the applicable standard, controlled access, encrypted transfer, no third-party tools.\n\nDashboards suppress small cells and always show denominators — partly for disclosure control, partly because a rate calculated on four patients is not a finding."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you tell us what caused a drop-off?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We can tell you what precedes it, how consistently, and in whom. That is association, and it is often enough to act on.\n\nWhat observational data cannot do is establish cause. Where that is the real question, the report says what design would answer it — which is a more useful answer than a confident one that does not hold."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Data analytics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/data-analytics-machine-learning/patient-journey-insights-machine-learning/"}]}];

